  "timingNotes": "Same performance as the DI take, re-amped through a Marshall Plexi sim. The two files are the same length and cross-correlate at a lag of +2.00ms (r=0.83-0.85 on a 300Hz-highpassed 1ms envelope), so these onsets are the DI onsets shifted by 2ms rather than an independent per-attack estimate on this file -- the amp sim's compression flattens the envelope far enough that a threshold-on-attack-peak measurement drifts on it. Onsets are measured on the DI file of this performance: a 1024/64 log-spectral-flux peak (150Hz-12kHz) matched one-to-one and in order against the events of the mic capture of the same take, then backtracked on a three-pole 1.5kHz-highpassed 1ms RMS envelope to local floor + 15% of the attack peak. The DI and the mic capture are the same performance -- cross-correlating their envelopes finds a constant offset of 923ms at a time-scale of exactly 1.000x -- and every one of the 48 labelled events has a distinct flux peak here, with a median disagreement of under 1ms against the mic label shifted by that offset. 3 of them (s14, s15, s34) had no flux peak within 45ms of where the beat puts them; those keep the mic label's onset shifted by the measured offset, which is itself an even subdivision of the surrounding beat rather than a measurement. End times are the next onset where the notes run together, and otherwise where the 20ms RMS falls below 15% of the note's attack peak. Note identity is inherited from the mic label of the same take and spot-checked here with a harmonic-sum f0 estimate, which agrees on all but the fastest run (and on the sixteenths only up to octave folding of the estimator). No detector output was used to derive any value here.",
